#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119564 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}$. Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= p + tv \\\\\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n${x = 1 - 2t; y = 4t; z =-2 + 3t$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$. Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 - 2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2 + 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1799/Source/Images/d43fa9b119feb6bcf4031525817fc87f.png)" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points\n\n$$\nP\\left(-1,1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ\\left(5,-1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨5,-1, 0⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6,-2, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-(-1)}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z-0}{0} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component is always maintains the constant value $z=0$. For example, our two points\n\n$$\nP\\left(-1,1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ\\left(5,-1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nboth have $z$-coordinates of $z=0$.\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $z=0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2},\\; z=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 4t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 + 5t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $y=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977be6c0-419a-5299-84c6-f7cf5904449a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117439} {:answer/id #uuid "9f91b4eb-88ca-56e2-9341-20c83a47edad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117440} {:answer/id #uuid "1071d965-b381-5d89-a6b2-e58fd56a8a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117441} {:answer/id #uuid "12ed9bc0-9edc-5061-927b-c67efa6a45e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117442} {:answer/id #uuid "14dceb70-f090-56c4-a2eb-f2cdd1a5c241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1071d965-b381-5d89-a6b2-e58fd56a8a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117441} :answer-field/id #uuid "62efd246-39ed-5e27-bd7f-655c5b62fc8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117438}] :question/id #uuid "96e088b3-5211-5361-9c40-899f5606d918" :question/math-academy-id "q-33004" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x}{1} = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = \\frac{z}{3}$. Find the vector equation of the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a03fc460-8b30-5116-95a4-a1379b48ccdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 7}{2} = \\frac{y + 4}{3}, z = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117577} {:answer/id #uuid "aedc3c5b-2001-5cee-a7cf-3d47e8dd8507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7} = \\frac{y}{-4} = \\frac{z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117578} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dac11f-00a7-5872-8616-18cfa31e8c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 2}{7} = \\frac{y - 3}{-4} = \\frac{z - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117579} {:answer/id #uuid "e32052cf-2e0b-5211-95e6-6b7935c21229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 2}{2} = \\frac{y - 3}{3} = \\frac{z - 1}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117580} {:answer/id #uuid "74db0c86-9c4e-5972-84ec-6572f57c17cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 7}{2} = \\frac{y + 4}{3} = \\frac{z - 3}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2dac11f-00a7-5872-8616-18cfa31e8c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 2}{7} = \\frac{y - 3}{-4} = \\frac{z - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117579} :answer-field/id #uuid "55656267-8b79-5816-951f-12b6d1fa6ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117576}] :question/id #uuid "deb4cbaf-5918-5a60-ba36-d2bbb72dfbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-33234" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1799/Source/Images/q-33234.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above."}]}
